{"name":"Drama Queen","hex":"#a37298","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"drama queen","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An overly or exaggeratedly dramatic person, especially a woman or a girl. Or a diva or a narcissist."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To behave in an exaggerately dramatic fashion."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/drama_queen"}],"entries":[{"title":"Drama Queen (Ivy Queen album)","blocks":[{"text":"Drama Queen is the seventh studio album by Puerto Rican reggaeton recording artist Ivy Queen. It was released on July 13, 2010, by Machete Music. The album was written by Queen with help from Rafael Castillo, Marcos Masis and others, while production was handled by Luny Tunes, Tainy and Noriega. The album features collaborations with De La Ghetto, Frank Reyes, Wisin & Yandel and Franco \"El Gorila\". It features a wide variety of musical styles in common with her previous album, Sentimiento, released three years earlier on a different label."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/e/e4/Ivy_Queen_-_Drama_Queen.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Drama_Queen_(Ivy_Queen_album)","wikidata":"Q4811175"},{"title":"Drama Queen (Neurosonic album)","blocks":[{"text":"Drama Queen is Canadian band Neurosonic's debut and only album. It peaked at number 9 on the Billboard Top Heatseekers album chart."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/b/b7/DramaQueen.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Drama_Queen_(Neurosonic_album)","wikidata":"Q5305736"},{"title":"Drama","blocks":[{"text":"Drama is the specific mode of fiction represented in performance: a play, opera, mime, ballet, etc., performed in a theatre, or on radio or television. Considered as a genre of poetry in general, the dramatic mode has been contrasted with the epic and the lyrical modes ever since Aristotle's Poetics —the earliest work of dramatic theory."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/The_Battle_of_Bosworth_Field_-_A_Scene_from_the_Great_Drama_of_History.jpg/330px-The_Battle_of_Bosworth_Field_-_A_Scene_from_the_Great_Drama_of_History.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Drama","wikidata":"Q25372"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/Wisin_%26_Yandel.jpg/500px-Wisin_%26_Yandel.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/Wisin_%26_Yandel.jpg/1280px-Wisin_%26_Yandel.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/Wisin_%26_Yandel.jpg/500px-Wisin_%26_Yandel.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Wisin & Yandel (pictured) collaborated and co-wrote \"Acércate\" with Ivy Queen and Marcos Masis.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWisin_%26_Yandel.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/Ivy_Queen_in_Studio.jpg/500px-Ivy_Queen_in_Studio.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/Ivy_Queen_in_Studio.jpg/1280px-Ivy_Queen_in_Studio.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/Ivy_Queen_in_Studio.jpg/500px-Ivy_Queen_in_Studio.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Ivy Queen before a promotional Walmart interview.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AIvy_Queen_in_Studio.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f6/Books_and_Scroll_Ornament_with_Open_Book.png/5